WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.777
(atmospheric sounds)

00:00:02.777 --> 00:00:04.416
- [Narrator] Sword-making has always

00:00:04.416 --> 00:00:06.295
been an esoteric endeavor. 

00:00:06.295 --> 00:00:09.995
There's never been a great
number of people doing it.

00:00:09.995 --> 00:00:12.091
(clangs)

00:00:12.091 --> 00:00:16.026
You have this transformation
of raw material,

00:00:16.026 --> 00:00:18.750
the alteration of nature,

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.051
and in the end, you get something

00:00:21.051 --> 00:00:23.523
that just exudes power.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:26.523
(suspenseful music)

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:35.369
- [Jon Snow] This is Valyrian steel.

00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:37.536
- [Man] My father's sword.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:40.872
- [Narrator] The ancient
swords on Game of Thrones,

00:00:40.872 --> 00:00:43.081
forged with Valyrian steel,

00:00:43.081 --> 00:00:46.364
were stronger and lighter
than any other weapon.

00:00:46.364 --> 00:00:49.444
No one knows how they were made.

00:00:49.444 --> 00:00:51.065
In the real world,

00:00:51.065 --> 00:00:53.954
over a thousand years ago in Viking lands,

00:00:53.954 --> 00:00:56.272
there was also a series of special swords

00:00:56.272 --> 00:00:59.102
with mysterious origins.

00:00:59.102 --> 00:01:02.704
And a blacksmith in
Wisconsin tried to make one.

00:01:02.704 --> 00:01:04.653
- This is an Ulfberht sword.

00:01:04.653 --> 00:01:07.457
(metal clanking)

00:01:07.457 --> 00:01:10.457
From about 850 to maybe early 1200s,

00:01:12.313 --> 00:01:14.312
in Viking-controlled lands,

00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:18.467
you had a sword that had a
name inlay called Ulfberht.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:22.165
Which roughly translates to, uh, 
"wolf tooth" or "wolf bear.”

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:24.082
It's the word of power.

00:01:25.478 --> 00:01:27.698
(atmospheric sounds)

00:01:27.698 --> 00:01:30.657
- [Narrator] There are only
350 known Ulfberht swords.

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.408
Forged with the quality of steel

00:01:32.408 --> 00:01:35.490
that didn't emerge in Europe
for another thousand years

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.630
no one knows exactly how they were made.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.484
- [Richard] I think
I've come fairly close,

00:01:40.484 --> 00:01:41.844
or at least a pretty good rendition

00:01:41.844 --> 00:01:44.438
of how they made those blades.

00:01:44.438 --> 00:01:47.498
Steel is a pretty special material.

00:01:47.498 --> 00:01:49.241
We know a great deal about it,

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:52.614
and we know almost nothing about it.

00:01:52.614 --> 00:01:55.879
So with the Ulfberht, you
have a mythical steel,

00:01:55.879 --> 00:01:57.530
mirrored in the Game of Thrones

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.287
where you have this steel that
no one's able to reproduce.

00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:02.717
That black Valyrian steel.

00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:06.336
Sword-making technology
still holds a bit of alchemy,

00:02:06.336 --> 00:02:08.029
a bit of mysticism in it.

00:02:08.029 --> 00:02:08.991
We want to believe that there's

00:02:08.991 --> 00:02:11.908
something that we can't understand.

00:02:13.524 --> 00:02:16.357
There's still mysteries in steel.

